From a103b59d6da03fe81f5e0567a7fa2d9964183a00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 16:43:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] firewall: Phase 2 egress defaults and Phase 3 hardened
 forwards

PHASE 2 - per-device egress defaults (reaper_fw_edef).
Zone policy already expresses "this network may not reach that network", but
zones are sets of INTERFACES, so it cannot say "this laptop gets no internet
unless a rule allows it" - every LAN device shares br0. Record is
enable>object>action>schedule, emitted into REAPER_FWF after the explicit rules
and before the zone policy, so precedence reads:

    protective head > explicit rules > per-device default > zone policy

An explicit allow therefore still beats that device's default deny, which is the
point of a default. Anchored to "-o $WANIF": a default-deny that also severed
the device from the LAN printer and the NAS would be a support call, not a
security control.

PHASE 3 - hardened inbound forwards (reaper_fw_nat), a new chain REAPER_FWN in
the NAT table hooked into PREROUTING. Unlike stock port forwarding these can be
restricted to a source object - including a geo object, which resolves to
Warden's country set - and scheduled. Each forward emits its DNAT and the
companion FORWARD ACCEPT together, because a DNAT into a default-deny FORWARD
path translates the packet and then drops it. IPv4 only, deliberately: DNAT is a
v4 NAT construct and the v6 way to publish a service is an ordinary ACCEPT rule,
which this engine already has.

Both lists join rfw_keys[], so the loader, the lastgood snapshot and the
rollback script pick them up and commit-confirm covers them with no extra work.
The nat table needs its own teardown - the filter-table loop cannot reach it,
and a REAPER_FWN left behind would keep DNATing after a disable or rollback.
$WANIF is resolved at run time like every other interface value and every
consumer is guarded with [ -n "$WANIF" ], so a box with no WAN emits no
egress/forward lines rather than unanchored ones.

DEFECT FOUND BY THE GENERATOR HARNESS, and it was latent in Phase 1:
rfw_emit_one_set() deliberately creates no v6 set for a MAC object (hash:mac has
no address family), but rfw_obj_set() still returned rwfw6_<name> on the v6 pass.
rfw_emit_rule() guards a DIRECT mac reference - a mac reached through a GROUP
never passed that check, so a group mixing a laptop and a phone-by-MAC emitted an
ip6tables rule matching a set that was never created. Fixed centrally in
rfw_obj_set(), which fixes rules, the new egress defaults and anything added
later at once; callers already treat "resolved to nothing" as "do not emit"
rather than "emit without the match", so the failure mode is a missing rule and
never an unrestricted one.

Harness-verified: correct precedence, group expansion, family-correct REJECT,
DNAT paired with its FORWARD ACCEPT, geo source resolving to rw_g_ru, four
deliberately-invalid forwards refused with reasons, zero cross-chain leaks and
zero duplicate lines.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
